Kernir patch 0011: the zip reader takes a zip64 locator with 0 total disks as a single disk

Upstream: libarchive 3.8.10, libarchive/archive_read_support_format_zip.c (read_zip64_eocd).

OneDrive and SharePoint write the zip64 end-of-central-directory locator of every download over 4 GB
with total-number-of-disks 0 (the spec says 1) and point the 32-bit end-of-central-directory record at
it with a central-directory offset of 0xFFFFFFFF. Upstream ignores such a locator, and in a file over
4 GiB the 0xFFFFFFFF offset passes read_eocd's bounds check, so the seekable reader wins the bid with
that offset and fails at once with "Damaged Zip archive" (system bsdtar and unzip fail the same way;
7-Zip and Python's zipfile read these files). 0 is now accepted like 1; more than 1 is still refused.

Apply from Sources/CLibArchive: patch -p1 < patches/0011-zip64-locator-zero-disks.patch

--- a/libarchive/archive_read_support_format_zip.c
+++ b/libarchive/archive_read_support_format_zip.c
@@ -4164,8 +4164,12 @@
 	/* Central dir must be on first volume. */
 	if (archive_le32dec(p + 4) != 0)
 		return 0;
-	/* Must be only a single volume. */
-	if (archive_le32dec(p + 16) != 1)
+	/* Must be only a single volume.
+	 * Kernir patch 0011: 0 counts as a single volume too. OneDrive and
+	 * SharePoint write 0 here in every download over 4 GB (with a 32-bit
+	 * EOCD whose CD offset is 0xFFFFFFFF); 7-Zip and Python's zipfile
+	 * read them. */
+	if (archive_le32dec(p + 16) > 1)
 		return 0;
 
 	/* Find the Zip64 EOCD record. */
